📅 Day 15 – Abstraction and Interfaces

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on the concepts of Abstraction and Interfaces in Java, which are important principles of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Abstraction

Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and showing only essential features of a class.

The mentor explained:

Use of abstract keyword

Abstract classes

Abstract methods (methods without body)

How child classes provide implementation

Key Points:

An abstract class cannot be instantiated.

It can contain both abstract and non-abstract methods.

Used when classes share common behavior but have different implementations.

2️⃣ Interfaces

An interface defines a contract that implementing classes must follow.

The lecture covered:

Declaration of interface using interface keyword

Methods in interface are implicitly public and abstract

Use of implements keyword

Multiple inheritance using interfaces

Key Points:

A class can implement multiple interfaces.

Interfaces provide full abstraction (before Java 8).

Used to achieve loose coupling and flexibility.

3️⃣ Difference Between Abstract Class and Interface

Abstract class can have constructors; interface cannot.

Abstract class can have both abstract and concrete methods; interface mainly contains abstract methods.

A class can extend only one abstract class but can implement multiple interfaces.

